#!/bin/sh
#
# A PUSH TO main IS A DEPLOY. THIS HOOK IS THE STOP.
#
# Anir, Aug 13 2026: "You can never, ever deploy it without my explicit
# permission. You can never make this mistake again. This is priority one."
#
# Writing it in CLAUDE.md and in memory was not enough — it had already been
# broken twice by an assistant reasoning its way to "he probably meant this
# push too". So the rule is enforced by the machine now: pushing to main is
# refused unless the person doing it states, in that command, that this
# specific push is approved.
#
#   FREYR_DEPLOY_APPROVED=yes git push origin HEAD:main
#
# Any other branch pushes normally. Nothing here can be satisfied by intent,
# by an earlier approval, or by a queue of work the owner asked for: the
# variable has to be typed for the push in hand.
#
# Wired up with:  git config core.hooksPath .githooks

protected="refs/heads/main"

while read -r _local_ref _local_sha remote_ref _remote_sha; do
  case "$remote_ref" in
    "$protected")
      if [ "$FREYR_DEPLOY_APPROVED" != "yes" ]; then
        cat >&2 <<'MSG'

  ✋ Push to main BLOCKED — this would deploy to production.

  .github/workflows/deploy.yml fires on every push to main and rolls ECS,
  which reaches the people using the app today.

  A push needs a yes from Anir for THIS push. Not an earlier "deploy when
  done", not "it is all work he asked for". Ask, name what is in it in one
  line, wait for the answer.

  When he has said yes to this specific push:

      FREYR_DEPLOY_APPROVED=yes git push origin HEAD:main

MSG
        exit 1
      fi
      ;;
  esac
done

exit 0
